---

[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, a few months ago I made a video on the Razer Tartarus. Then more recently I made a video on the Azeron Cyborg 2. And after that video went live, Martins and the team over at Azeron actually reached out to me and said that there was something they\'d like me to take a look'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at. And that was the Azeron Keypad. Now before we go any further, I do want to make it clear that Azeron did send this out to me. However, this video is not sponsored and Azeron do not get any input on what I\'m about to say. It is based exclusively off of my experience'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the device over the last 4 to 6 weeks. Now with that said, I do also want to say that I now have an affiliate code with Azeron, code Big Winter at checkout if you do decide to pick one up for 5% off and to support the channel. But as always, my aim is to give my'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'honest thoughts regardless. Now if you\'ve ever looked at one of these things or anything like it, you probably have a couple of questions. What actually is it? How long would something like this take to learn? Is it actually better than a normal keyboard? And'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ultimately, is it worth the money? So after spending the last month or so gaming and using it more generally, let\'s talk about it. So first things first, what actually is this thing? [music] Because unlike a traditional'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gaming keypad, the Keypad is trying to do something a little bit different. At its core, it\'s Azeron\'s attempt to make a more ergonomic gaming keyboard. You\'ve got Cherry MX switches in your choice of red, brown, or blue. You\'ve got a hall'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'effect thumb stick. You\'ve got 32 mappable inputs, DSA keycaps, and up to six onboard profiles with many more software. But the biggest difference compared to a normal keyboard is that movement is designed around the thumb'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stick. You can absolutely bind movement to the keys if you want to, like WASD, but I think most people are probably just going to end up using the thumb stick. And honestly, I think that\'s where a lot of the appeal comes from'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with a device like this. If you\'re coming from keyboard and mouse, yes, it gives you something different. If you\'re coming from controller, you\'ve immediately got something familiar sitting under your thumb. And it sits somewhere between those two inputs, and'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that is exactly what Azeron was aiming for. So first impressions, when this thing first arrived, I was actually very excited to get my hands on it because I actually nearly bought the Keyzender over the Cyborg 2 in the first place. And after spending time with other'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gaming keypads, I was very curious to see what Azeron\'s take would be on a keyboard replacement and what that would feel like. And one thing I noticed immediately though, was that it actually felt far more familiar than I\'d initially expected. The Tartarus still'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'felt mostly like keyboard. The Cyborg felt like learning an entirely new device. And the Keyzender at first glance is somewhere between those two. And I think that\'s actually one of its biggest strengths because despite'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looking unusual, you already understand most of what you\'re looking at. You\'ve got keyboard switches, a thumb stick, and a vaguely familiar layout. And because of that, it doesn\'t feel nearly as intimidating as something like the'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Cyborg did when I first took it out of the box. Now, that doesn\'t mean that there isn\'t a learning curve. There definitely is, but I think most people are going to feel less overwhelmed on day one than they might expect it. Now, one of the biggest questions I tend to'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'find people have with a device like this is how long does it actually take to learn? And honestly, I think it takes a lot less time than people expect it to. Now, I should probably acknowledge that by the time I got the Keyzender, I wasn\'t exactly starting from zero. I'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spent years gaming on keyboard and mouse. Then I moved to the Tartarus for 18-plus months. Then I spent the last six or so weeks with the Cyborg. [music] So, by the time the Keyzender arrived, I was already used to the idea of moving'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with my thumb rather than with my fingers and that kind of layout. For most people though, you\'re probably coming directly from either a keyboard or a controller. And honestly, I don\'t think either is necessarily a huge disadvantage. If you\'re coming from'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'controller, you\'ve already spent years moving with your thumb. So, that part actually feels surprisingly natural. If you\'re coming from keyboard and mouse, the thumb stick is obviously the biggest adjustment, but because the overall layout and interaction feels much closer'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to a traditional keyboard than it might initially look, I actually think most people would adapt pretty quickly. For me, the first few days were mostly about figuring out where everything lived, not necessarily how to use the device. And'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'d honestly say, if you are playing regularly, you\'ll probably feel comfortable within a week. And if you\'re putting in serious time into games, I\'d expect most people to have proper muscle memory built up by the end of the second'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'week. Because despite how unusual this might look, the Keyzen is actually a lot more intuitive than it appears. And I think another question that people naturally tend to ask is, will it actually make me better at games? And'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'honestly, no. I don\'t think so, at least not at first. In fact, I\'d actually argue the opposite. For the first few days, maybe even that first week, you\'re probably going to play a little bit'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'worse. You\'re building completely new muscle memory. You\'re thinking about where buttons are. You\'re probably second-guessing yourself, and that\'s completely normal. But once that learning curve is out of the way, I don\'t think the Keyzen magically makes'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you a better player. I think it allows you to play to your own potential much more comfortably. Everything is close to your fingers. Your hand is in a much more natural position, and you\'ve got your movement just under your thumb. And'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because of that, I find myself thinking less about my inputs and more about the game itself. So, no. I don\'t think buying a Keyzen suddenly makes you cracked at Apex overnight. But once you\'ve adapted to it, I do think it\'s a'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more comfortable and ergonomic way to play for longer sessions. Now, the next question we\'re going to take a look at is, isn\'t it a pain to set up? To be honest with you, yeah. It is a little bit. But it is worth taking the time to do it properly. Because once you'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do have it dialed in, you won\'t actually have to adjust it again. One thing I think is easy to miss when looking at photos of the Keyzen online is just how adjustable it actually is. For each finger, you can adjust the width between'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the rails and the depth of those rails. So, you\'re not just performing hand acrobatics to reach around the device, you\'re actually fitting the device to your hand. You can also adjust the thumb stick position, its angle, and the'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'placement of the buttons surrounding it. So, there is quite a bit of flexibility to dial things into your liking. Now, if you watched my Cyborg video, which probably be on screen right now, the Cyborg still has the edge when it comes to pure adjustability. That device just'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'allows adjustment in a couple of extra dimensions. But, honestly, the KeyZane is still incredibly customizable compared to pretty much anything else on the market. Now, Azeron actually sent me both the flat and the curved palm rests.'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ve mainly been using the flat version with the KeyZane so far, and initially wasn\'t so sure about it. The hand position feels different to what I was expecting, and honestly, what I was used to with the curved palm rest I was using'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the Cyborg. Your hand definitely sits much flatter, it\'s more open, and because of that, some of the outer buttons initially felt further away than I\'d expected. The best comparison that I can give, I think, is reaching some of'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'those buttons feels a bit like reaching towards the F keys or the number row on a keyboard. It\'s not exactly difficult, it\'s just a bit different. In fact, one of my biggest criticisms during the first few days was I thought the device'

[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'felt a bit too big, even though I\'d ordered the correct size for my meat mitts. [music] And as it turns out, yeah, that was that was that was my bad. I was positioning my hand far too far back on the palm rest. Once I started'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'moving my hand further inwards and spent the time building the muscle memory, that problem actually completely disappeared. And overall, it\'s been extremely comfortable. No hand cramps, wrist pain, shoulder discomfort, which'

[07:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is exactly what I\'d want from something that\'s trying to improve on the traditional keyboard experience. Azeron also were gracious enough to send me their 3D printed rest as well, which allows you to elevate the device'

[07:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slightly more, and also change the angle of it as well to better suit your ergonomics. Personally, I haven\'t actually been using it very much and I didn\'t use it much on the Cyborg either because I just found both devices really'

[07:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comfortable without them. Now, one of my favorite things about the Keyzenn is definitely the thumbstick and I think it\'s the biggest reason why most people would consider buying one in the first place, particularly if you\'re coming from controller and you want to move to'

[08:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'PC because you\'ve immediately got something familiar that you can ground yourself with. Like I said, you can use it in analog mode like a controller or you can use it in WASD mode. For me, I\'ve continued using it in that WASD'

[08:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'format, mainly because I play a lot of Apex, Marathon, other shooters where I like the snappy responsiveness. I did spend some time trying analog movement as well and whilst it definitely felt'

[08:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more natural in some games, I still found myself gravitating back towards WASD because it\'s what I\'m used to after years of PC gaming. Plus, it depends on the game, but some games actually don\'t support both analog mode and keyboard'

[08:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inputs and in certain games you\'ll see that the glyphs will switch between controller and keyboard inputs and it can be a little distracting sometimes. So, check the game before you set your profile. And one more thing I do want to'

[08:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mention is the thumbstick itself. I deliberately chose the standard thumbstick for the Keyzenn rather than one of the upgraded options because I wanted to experience what most people are likely to buy and I actually used'

[09:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the upgraded thumbstick on the Cyborg already. And whilst it is perfectly good, it doesn\'t feel quite as premium as I would have hoped. On hotter days especially, there has been a heatwave in the UK, it has been miserable, I noticed it could feel a little bit slick if your'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hands weren\'t perfectly dry. Now, it\'s not a deal-breaker by any means, but it\'s something worth mentioning. The material is closer to a 3D printed resin rather than this harder rubber and metal'

[09:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you get on the premium version and I much prefer that one. Now, in terms of the Azeron software, it\'s actually pretty straightforward. You have full remapping, you have macros, multiple profiles, onboard memory of up to six'

[09:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'profiles, and many more within the software. Everything that you would expect. But honestly, the thing that helped me the most wasn\'t necessarily any fancy software features, it was just consistency. What I ended up doing was'

[09:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'giving each finger its own job. For example, jump is always going to be in the same place. Sprint, crouch, reload, and other common actions. So, no matter what game I\'m playing, it all can fit'

[10:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into one default profile. So, when I do play a new game, I\'m not relearning controls every single time. I\'m just moving game-specific abilities around that default template. I think it\'s one of the biggest reasons why I happened to'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adapt to the Key Zen so quickly, because muscle memory isn\'t [music] really about remembering buttons, it\'s about teaching each finger a role. And whilst you can automatically swap profiles based on the game or the program that you\'re running,'

[10:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I like my default approach. But feel free to check the Azeron Discord as well, as people tend to share their profiles to be [music] imported in there all of the time, and they\'re pretty good. And another thing that I tend to see online with Azeron products in'

[10:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'general is the big question, isn\'t it just 3D printed? To which I will say, yes, it is 3D printed, and in certain places you can most definitely tell. But just like with the Cyborg, the overall package feels excellent. You\'ve got'

[10:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tools for all of your adjustments, you\'ve got spare parts, different accessories, and plenty [music] of customization options. And whilst there is a little movement in some places, I genuinely think that\'s more of a'

[11:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'consequence of it being adjustable, rather than poor 3D build quality. And finally, the biggest question of them all, is it actually worth the money? Because Azeron products are not cheap. And for me,'

[11:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yes, I think it is. But not because I think you should throw away your keyboard, especially cuz you\'re going to need one in your day-to-day life anyway. And not because it is some magical device that is going to instantly make'

[11:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you a better player. I think it\'s worth it because it solves a specific problem. If you\'re perfectly happy on keyboard or controller, you probably don\'t have that problem, and you probably don\'t need one. But, if you\'ve ever found yourself'

[11:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wanting something more ergonomic, something more comfortable, and something that bridges the gap between controller and keyboard a little better, then the Keyzender is a really interesting option here. And to be honest with you, the longer I\'ve used'

[11:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it, the more I\'ve liked it. Now, if we\'re looking beyond the Keyzender, I\'m sure there are probably a couple of you out there with a very similar question. How does it stack up compared to other gaming keypads that I\'ve tried? And well, that\'s actually exactly what a'

[12:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'couple of my next videos are going to be about. Because I\'ve now spent meaningful time with the Razer Tartarus, the Azeron Keyzender, and the Azeron Cyborg. So, the next step is putting them'

[12:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'head-to-head and figuring out which one I think is right [music] for different types of players. Because I don\'t think there\'s necessarily one perfect answer for everybody. Now, once again, a massive thank you to the team over at Azeron for sending me over the Keyzender'

[12:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to take a look at. And remember to use code Big Winter at checkout for 5% off if you do end up picking up any of the Azeron devices. And if you have any questions about the Keyzender and or the Cyborg, feel free to put them in the'

[12:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comments and I\'ll get back to you as best I can. And if you do want to see those comparison videos, make sure that you\'re subscribed and ding that bell to be notified when they go live. Drop a like if you enjoyed the video and subscribe for more tech and gaming content. Thank you so much for watching.'

[12:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Take care out there, and much love.'
